        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>[Acupoint Location, Pinyin Label, Chinese Label] WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization (WHO), WHO Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 108. https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/353407</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>[Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, Indications] Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 8, Acupuncture Points of the Shaoyin and Taiyang Meridians. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <tara:hasChineseLabel>膈兪(俞)</tara:hasChineseLabel>
        <tara:hasGeneralSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0008231"/>
        <tara:hasIndicationsDescription>vomiting, hiccup, belching, difficulty in swallowing, asthma, cough, spitting of blood, afternoon fever, night sweat, measles.</tara:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <tara:hasInnervationDescription>The medial branches of the posterior rami of the seventh and eighth thoracic nerves; deeper, their lateral branches.</tara:hasInnervationDescription>
        <tara:hasLocationalDescription>In the upper back region, at the same level as the inferior border of the spinous process of the seventh thoracic vertebra (T7), 1.5 B-cun lateral to the posterior median line. Note: The inferior angle of the scapula is at the same level as the spinous process of the seventh thoracic vertebra.</tara:hasLocationalDescription>
        <tara:hasMethodDescription>Puncture obliquely 0.5-0.7 inch. Moxibustion is applicable.</tara:hasMethodDescription>
        <tara:hasPinyinLabel>Geshu</tara:hasPinyinLabel>
        <tara:hasSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_9763906"/>
        <tara:hasSpecificSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0004632"/>
        <tara:hasSpecificSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0006849"/>
        <tara:hasSpecificSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795312"/>
        <tara:hasSynonym>B 17</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasSynonym>Bladder 17</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasSynonym>Influential Point of Blood</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasSynonym>UB 17</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasSynonym>Urinary Bladder 17</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasVasculatureDescription>The medial branches of the posterior branches of the intercostal artery and vein.</tara:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <tara:isLocatedOnMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7525476"/>
        <rdfs:label>BL 17</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_1132428 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_1132428">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000024"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A fiat object part of the human body, delimited by the practice of needling rather than by any natural physical discontinuity, consisting of a surface locus where a needle is inserted together with the shallow underlying tissue it penetrates, without commitment to the exact depth or angle of penetration.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dc:description>According to traditional Chinese medicine (TCM) theory, acupoints are specifically chosen sites of acupuncture manipulation, and also the basis for studying the mechanism of acupuncture. Stimulating different acupoints on the body surface could provide various therapeutic benefits.</dc:description>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Fang Li, Tian He, Qian Xu, Li-Ting Lin, Hui Li, Yan Liu, Guang-Xia Shi, Cun-Zhi Liu, What is the Acupoint? A preliminary review of Acupoints, Pain Medicine, Volume 16, Issue 10, October 2015, Pages 1905–1915, https://doi.org/10.1111/pme.12761</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <tara:hasSynonym>Acupuncture Point</tara:hasSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>Acupoint</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


