        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>[Acupoint Location, Pinyin Label, Chinese Label] WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization (WHO), WHO Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 224. https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/353407</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>[Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, Indications] Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 10, Acupuncture Points of the Governor and the Conception Vessels and the Extra Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <tara:hasChineseLabel>下脘</tara:hasChineseLabel>
        <tara:hasGeneralSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795301"/>
        <tara:hasIndicationsDescription>epigastric pain, abdominal pain, borborygmus, indigestion, vomiting, diarrhea.</tara:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <tara:hasInnervationDescription>The anterior cutaneous branch of the eighth intercostal nerve.</tara:hasInnervationDescription>
        <tara:hasLocationalDescription>On the upper abdomen, 2 B-cun superior to the centre of the umbilicus, on the anterior median line.</tara:hasLocationalDescription>
        <tara:hasMethodDescription>Puncture perpendicularly 0.5-1.2 inches. Moxibustion is applicable.</tara:hasMethodDescription>
        <tara:hasPinyinLabel>Xiawan</tara:hasPinyinLabel>
        <tara:hasSpecificSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0007118"/>
        <tara:hasSpecificSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795285"/>
        <tara:hasSynonym>Conception Vessel 10</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasSynonym>RN 10</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasSynonym>Ren 10</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasSynonym>VC 10</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasVasculatureDescription>The inferior epigastric artery and vein.</tara:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <tara:isLocatedOnMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6789717"/>
        <rdfs:label>CV 10</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_1840369 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_1840369">
        <owl:equivalentClass>
            <owl:Class>
                <owl:intersectionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
                    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4943316"/>
                    <owl:Restriction>
                        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/object-property/hasSpecialPointRole"/>
                        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_3455429"/>
                    </owl:Restriction>
                </owl:intersectionOf>
            </owl:Class>
        </owl:equivalentClass>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4943316"/>
        <dc:description>Each of the twelve main meridians has a Yuan-Primary Point, which is located on the limbs. The Chinese character (Yuan) means primary qi in this context. The chapter &quot;The 66th Medical Problem&quot; in Classic of Medical Problems describes the relation between the Yuan-Primary Points and Yuan-Primary Qi. The Yuan-Primary Qi, originating below the umbilicus and between the kidneys, is dispersed to the zang-fu organs and further to the limbs via Triple Energizer. The sites where the Yuan-Primary Qi is retained are Yuan-Primary Points, which are used to treat disorders of the zang-fu organs. In the yin meridians, the Yuan-Primary Poims overlap with the Shu-Stream Points of the Five Shu Points. Each yang meridian, however, has its Yuan-Primary Point other than the Shu-Stream Point.</dc:description>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <tara:hasSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_3455429"/>
        <rdfs:label>Yuan-Primary Point</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


