        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>[Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, Indications] Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 9, Acupuncture Points of the Jueyin and Shaoyang Meridians. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <tara:hasChineseLabel>懸(悬)鍾(钟)</tara:hasChineseLabel>
        <tara:hasGeneralSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0003823"/>
        <tara:hasIndicationsDescription>apoplexy, hemiplegia, pain of the neck, abdominal distension, pain in the hypochondriac region, muscular atrophy of the lower limbs, spastic pain of the leg, beriberi.</tara:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <tara:hasLocationalDescription>On the fibular aspect of the leg, anterior to the fibula, 3 B-cun proximal to the prominence of the lateral malleolus.</tara:hasLocationalDescription>
        <tara:hasMethodDescription>Puncture perpendicularly 0.3-0.5 inch. Moxibustion is applicable.</tara:hasMethodDescription>
        <tara:hasPinyinLabel>Xuanzhong</tara:hasPinyinLabel>
        <tara:hasSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_9924428"/>
        <tara:hasSpecificSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0001446"/>
        <tara:hasSpecificSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0012291"/>
        <tara:hasSynonym>G 39</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasSynonym>Gallbladder 39</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasVasculatureDescription>The branches of the anterior tibial artery and vein.</tara:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <tara:isLocatedOnMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_5283975"/>
        <rdfs:label>GB 39</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_3779268 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_3779268">
        <owl:equivalentClass>
            <owl:Class>
                <owl:intersectionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
                    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7819406"/>
                    <owl:Restriction>
                        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/object-property/hasSpecialPointRole"/>
                        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2392868"/>
                    </owl:Restriction>
                </owl:intersectionOf>
            </owl:Class>
        </owl:equivalentClass>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7819406"/>
        <dc:description>The Major Luo-Connecting Point of the Spleen is a Major Luo-Connecting Point. Dabao (SP 21) is the Major Luo-Connecting Point of the Spleen. Each of twelve main meridians has, on the limbs, a Luo-Connecting Point to link its exteriorly-interiorly related meridian. Each of the Governor and Conception vessels, and the Major Collateral of the spleen has its Luo-Connecting Point on the trunk. A Luo-Connecting Point is used to treat disorders involving the two exteriorly-interiorly related meridians and those in the area supplied by the two meridians.</dc:description>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <tara:hasSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2392868"/>
        <rdfs:label>Major Luo-Connecting Point of the Spleen</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


