        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>[Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, Indications] Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 9, Acupuncture Points of the Jueyin and Shaoyang Meridians. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <tara:hasChineseLabel>天泉</tara:hasChineseLabel>
        <tara:hasGeneralSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0001460"/>
        <tara:hasIndicationsDescription>cardiac pain, distension of the hypochondriac region, cough, pain in the chest, back and the medial aspect of the arm.</tara:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <tara:hasInnervationDescription>The medial brachial cutaneous nerve and the musculocutaneous nerve.</tara:hasInnervationDescription>
        <tara:hasLocationalDescription>On the anterior aspect of the arm, between the long head and short head of the biceps brachii muscle, 2 B-cun distal to the anterior axillary fold.</tara:hasLocationalDescription>
        <tara:hasMethodDescription>Puncture perpendicularly 0.5-0.7 inch. Moxibustion is applicable.</tara:hasMethodDescription>
        <tara:hasPinyinLabel>Tianquan</tara:hasPinyinLabel>
