        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>[Acupoint Location, Pinyin Label, Chinese Label] WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization (WHO), WHO Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 40. https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/353407</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>[Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, Indications] Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 7, Acupuncture Points of the Taiyin and Yangming Meridians. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <tara:hasChineseLabel>手五里</tara:hasChineseLabel>
        <tara:hasGeneralSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0001460"/>
        <tara:hasIndicationsDescription>contracture and pain of the elbow and arm, scrofula.</tara:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <tara:hasInnervationDescription>The posterior antebrachial cutaneous nerve; deeper, the radial nerve.</tara:hasInnervationDescription>
        <tara:hasLocationalDescription>On the lateral aspect of the arm, on the line connecting LI11 with LI15, 3 B-cun supe- rior to the cubital crease.</tara:hasLocationalDescription>
        <tara:hasMethodDescription>Puncture perpendicularly 0.5-1.0 inch. Avoid injuring the artery.</tara:hasMethodDescription>
        <tara:hasPinyinLabel>Shouwuli</tara:hasPinyinLabel>
        <tara:hasRelatedArtery rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0797497"/>
        <tara:hasRelatedNerve rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0001492"/>
        <tara:hasRelatedNerve rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0797477"/>
        <tara:hasRelatedVein rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0797528"/>
        <tara:hasSpecificSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795332"/>
        <tara:hasSynonym>CO 13</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasSynonym>Colon 13</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasSynonym>Large Intestine 13</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasVasculatureDescription>The radial collateral artery and vein.</tara:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <tara:isLocatedOnMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4610474"/>
        <rdfs:label>LI 13</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_5629511 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_5629511">
        <owl:equivalentClass>
            <owl:Class>
                <owl:intersectionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
                    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_5151019"/>
                    <owl:Restriction>
                        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/object-property/isLocatedOnMeridian"/>
                        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4167850"/>
                    </owl:Restriction>
                </owl:intersectionOf>
            </owl:Class>
        </owl:equivalentClass>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_5151019"/>
        <dc:description>An acupuncture point that is located along the Spleen Meridian of Foot-Taiyin (SP), which consists of 21 standardized acupoints beginning at SP 1 (Yinbai) on the medial side of the great toe near the corner of the nail, traveling up along the medial aspect of the leg and continuing up the abdomen and chest, and ending at SP 21 (Dabao) on the lateral thorax in the sixth intercostal space on the midaxillary line.</dc:description>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization (WHO), WHO Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/353407</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <tara:hasSynonym>Acupoint of the spleen meridian of foot-taiyin</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasSynonym>Acupuncture point of the spleen meridian</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasSynonym>Acupuncture point of the spleen meridian of foot-taiyin</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasSynonym>SP</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:isLocatedOnMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4167850"/>
        <rdfs:label>Acupoint of the Spleen Meridian</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_5653254 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_5653254">
        <owl:equivalentClass>
            <owl:Class>
                <owl:intersectionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
                    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4830205"/>
                    <owl:Restriction>
                        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/object-property/hasSpecialPointRole"/>
                        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_0513340"/>
                    </owl:Restriction>
                </owl:intersectionOf>
            </owl:Class>
        </owl:equivalentClass>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4830205"/>
        <dc:description>The Lower He-Sea Point of the the Gallbladder meridian. One of the five Shu Points, the He-Sea point signifies the confluence of rivers in the sea, where the meridian qi is the most flourishing. In addition, each of the six fu organs has another He-Sea point in the three yang meridians of the foot, known as the Lower He-Sea point. The Lower He-Sea points are mostly employed to treat the disorders of the six fu organs in clinic.</dc:description>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <tara:hasSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_0513340"/>
        <rdfs:label>Lower He-Sea Point of the Gallbladder</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


