        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>[Acupoint Location, Pinyin Label, Chinese Label] WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization (WHO), WHO Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 182. https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/353407</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>[Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, Indications] Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 9, Acupuncture Points of the Jueyin and Shaoyang Meridians. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <tara:hasChineseLabel>淵(渊)液</tara:hasChineseLabel>
        <tara:hasGeneralSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795306"/>
        <tara:hasIndicationsDescription>fullness in the chest, swelling of the axillary region, pain in the hypochondriac region, pain and motor impairment of the arm.</tara:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <tara:hasInnervationDescription>The lateral cutaneous branch of the fifth intercostal nerve, the branch of the long thoracic nerve.</tara:hasInnervationDescription>
        <tara:hasLocationalDescription>In the lateral thoracic region, in the fourth intercostal space, on the midaxillary line.</tara:hasLocationalDescription>
        <tara:hasMethodDescription>Puncture obliquely 0.3-0.5 inch.</tara:hasMethodDescription>
        <tara:hasPinyinLabel>Yuanye</tara:hasPinyinLabel>
        <tara:hasSpecificSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795298"/>
        <tara:hasSpecificSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795308"/>
        <tara:hasSynonym>G 22</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasSynonym>Gallbladder 22</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasVasculatureDescription>The thoracoepigastric vein, the lateral thoracic artery and vein, the fifth intercostal artery and vein.</tara:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <tara:isLocatedOnMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_5283975"/>
        <rdfs:label>GB 22</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6119170 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6119170">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7226516"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A Meridian, other than the twelve principal meridians, that is not directly affiliated with a zang or fu organ system and does not stand in an exterior-interior pairing with another meridian.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dc:description>Represents the extra meridians. The eight extra meridians are the Governor Vessel, Conception Vessel, Thoroughfare Vessel, Belt Vessel, Yang Heel Vessel, Yin Heel Vessel, Yang Link Vessel and Yin Link Vessel. They are different from the twelve main meridians because none of them pertains to the zang organs and communicates with the fu organs of pertains to the fu organs and communicates with the zang organs. And they are not exteriorly-interiorly related. Apart from the Governor Vessel and Conception Vessel which have their own acupuncture points, the extra meridians share their points with other main meridians.</dc:description>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Page 82. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <rdfs:label>Extra Meridian</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


