        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>[Acupoint Location, Pinyin Label, Chinese Label] WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization (WHO), WHO Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 109. https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/353407</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>[Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, Indications] Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 8, Acupuncture Points of the Shaoyin and Taiyang Meridians. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <tara:hasChineseLabel>膽兪(俞)</tara:hasChineseLabel>
        <tara:hasGeneralSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0008231"/>
        <tara:hasIndicationsDescription>jaundice, bitter taste of the mouth, pain in the chest and hypochondriac region, pulmonary tuberculosis, afternoon fever.</tara:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <tara:hasInnervationDescription>The medial cutaneous branches of the posterior rami of the tenth and eleventh thoracic nerves; deeper, their lateral branches.</tara:hasInnervationDescription>
        <tara:hasLocationalDescription>In the upper back region, at the same level as the inferior border of the spinous process of the tenth thoracic vertebra (T10), 1.5 B- cun lateral to the posterior median line.</tara:hasLocationalDescription>
        <tara:hasMethodDescription>Puncture obliquely 0.5-0.8 inch. Moxibustion is applicable.</tara:hasMethodDescription>
        <tara:hasPinyinLabel>Danshu</tara:hasPinyinLabel>
        <tara:hasSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_1149283"/>
        <tara:hasSpecificSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0004634"/>
        <tara:hasSpecificSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795312"/>
        <tara:hasSynonym>B 19</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasSynonym>Back-Shu Point of the Gallbladder</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasSynonym>Bladder 19</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasSynonym>UB 19</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasSynonym>Urinary Bladder 19</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasVasculatureDescription>The medial branches of the posterior branches of the intercostal artery and vein.</tara:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <tara:isLocatedOnMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7525476"/>
        <rdfs:label>BL 19</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6173621 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6173621">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7571236"/>
        <rdfs:label>Front-Mu Point of the Liver Role</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6200120 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6200120">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7226516"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>One of the twelve Meridians that is directly affiliated with a zang or fu organ system via hasAssociatedOrgan, and that is linked to the other eleven principal meridians in a fixed circulatory sequence.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dc:description>As the major part in the meridian system, the twelve main meridians share the following features. Each with its own acupuncture points is distributed on a fixed portion of the body surface; each pertains to either a zang or a fu organ (those that pertain to the zang organ communicating with the fu organ, and vice versa). 

The twelve main meridians link one another in a fixed order. A cyclical now of qi is maintained by the connection of the meridians of the hand and foot, yin and yang, exterior and interior.</dc:description>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>D. Liangyue, G. Yijun, H. Shuhui, et al. Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Revised ed. Beijing: Foreign Languages Press; 1999. Page 65. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <tara:hasSynonym>Primary meridian</tara:hasSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>Main Meridian</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6203934 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6203934">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_9507527"/>
        <rdfs:label>Back-Shu Point of the Gallbladder Role</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6220650 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6220650">
        <owl:equivalentClass>
            <owl:Class>
                <owl:intersectionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
                    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_8746709"/>
                    <owl:Restriction>
                        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/object-property/hasSpecialPointRole"/>
                        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_9515358"/>
                    </owl:Restriction>
                </owl:intersectionOf>
            </owl:Class>
        </owl:equivalentClass>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_8746709"/>
        <dc:description>The Front-Mu Point of the Heart meridian. Front-Mu points are those points on the chest and abdomen where the qi of the respective zang-fu organs is infused and converged. Located close to their corresponding zang-fu organs, the Front-Mu points play a significant role in the diagnosis and treatment of the disorders of the internal organs.</dc:description>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <tara:hasSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_9515358"/>
        <rdfs:label>Front-Mu Point of the Heart</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


