                        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795500"/>
                    </owl:Restriction>
                </owl:someValuesFrom>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>[Acupoint Location, Pinyin Label, Chinese Label] WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations in the Western Pacific Region. World Health Organization (WHO), WHO Regional Office for the Western Pacific, 2008. Page 154. https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/353407</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>[Acupuncture Method, Vasculature, Innervation, Indications] Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 9, Acupuncture Points of the Jueyin and Shaoyang Meridians. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <tara:hasChineseLabel>間(间)使</tara:hasChineseLabel>
        <tara:hasGeneralSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0002386"/>
        <tara:hasIndicationsDescription>cardiac pain, palpitation, stomachache, vomiting, febrile diseases, irritability, malaria, mental disorder, epilepsy, swelling of the axilla, contracture of the elbow and arm.</tara:hasIndicationsDescription>
        <tara:hasInnervationDescription>The medial and lateral antebrachial cutaneous nerves, the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve; deeper, the anterior interosseous nerve.</tara:hasInnervationDescription>
        <tara:hasLocationalDescription>On the anterior aspect of the forearm, between the tendons of the palmaris longus and the flexor carpi radialis, 3 B-cun proximal to the palmar wrist crease. Note 1: With the fist clenched, the wrist supinated and the elbow slightly flexed, the two tendons become more prominent. PC5 is located 3 B-cun proximal to PC7. Note 2: If the palmaris longus tendon is not present, PC5 is medial to the flexor carpi radialis tendon.</tara:hasLocationalDescription>
        <tara:hasMethodDescription>Puncture perpendicularly 0.5-1.0 inch. Moxibustion is applicable.</tara:hasMethodDescription>
        <tara:hasPinyinLabel>Jianshi</tara:hasPinyinLabel>
        <tara:hasSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7315457"/>
        <tara:hasSpecificSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0016496"/>
        <tara:hasSpecificSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795334"/>
        <tara:hasSpecificSurfaceLocation rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0795500"/>
        <tara:hasSynonym>P 5</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasSynonym>Pericardium 5</tara:hasSynonym>
        <tara:hasVasculatureDescription>The median artery and vein; deeper, the anterior interosseous artery and vein.</tara:hasVasculatureDescription>
        <tara:isLocatedOnMeridian rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4694897"/>
        <rdfs:label>PC 5</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6484209 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6484209">
        <owl:equivalentClass>
            <owl:Class>
                <owl:intersectionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
                    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_3861175"/>
                    <owl:Restriction>
                        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/object-property/hasSpecialPointRole"/>
                        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2579465"/>
                    </owl:Restriction>
                </owl:intersectionOf>
            </owl:Class>
        </owl:equivalentClass>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_3861175"/>
        <dc:description>The Back-Shu Point of the Bladder meridian. Back-Shu points are specific points on the back where the qi of the respective zang-fu organs is infused. Situated close to their respectively related zang-fu organs, the Back-Shu points present abnormal reactions to the dysfunction of their corresponding zang-fu organs. They are often used for disorders of the internal organs.</dc:description>
        <dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
        <tara:hasSpecialPointRole rdf:resource="http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2579465"/>
        <rdfs:label>Back-Shu Point of the Bladder</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6499349 -->

